            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Replaces each value of a tensor with the running total up to and including it: the first value, then the first two added, then the first three, and so on.\n\nA sum gives you the total; a cumulative sum gives you the total SO FAR at every point. Use it to turn steps into a path (movements into positions, speeds into distance travelled), to turn a histogram into a cumulative distribution, or to turn a list of durations into start times.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.cumsum <dim: int>\nDefault dim 0, the FIRST axis. For a 1-D tensor that is the only axis;\nfor a 2-D tensor it runs DOWN each column - unlike np.cumsum, which runs\nalong each row by default. Give an axis - t.cumsum 1, or -1 for the last\n- and a dim box appears so it can be changed.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.cumsum\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or NumPy array is turned into a float32 tensor). Every input is summed and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send. An axis the tensor does not have sends nothing.\n\ndim (int box, only with an argument):\nWhich axis to run along. Negative counts from the end. Changing it does not send by itself; the next tensor uses it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe running totals, the same shape as the input. The last value is the total of the whole run (what t.sum would give for a 1-D tensor).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: t.rand makes 200 random steps between -1 and 1, a new set every half second. The upper plot shows them - noise, with no shape. The lower plot is their cumsum: a random walk, the path you get by taking those steps one after another. Drag t.rand's max up to 1.2 (or min down) and the steps are no longer balanced: the walk drifts steadily up (or down).\n\nBottom: ten 1s summed give the ramp 1 2 3 ... 10 - the cumsum of a constant is a straight line whose slope is the constant. Edit the message (2 2 2, or 1 -1 1 -1) and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.cumsum is the NumPy version. t.diff is the reverse: it turns a path back into its steps. t.cumprod multiplies instead of adding. t.sum gives only the final total. accumulate keeps a running total of a STREAM, one number at a time.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 810,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Replaces each value of a tensor with the running total up to and including it: the first value, then the first two added, then the first three, and so on.\n\nA sum gives you the total; a cumulative sum gives you the total SO FAR at every point. Use it to turn steps into a path (movements into positions, speeds into distance travelled), to turn a histogram into a cumulative distribution, or to turn a list of durations into start times.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.cumsum <dim: int>\nDefault dim 0, the FIRST axis. For a 1-D tensor that is the only axis;\nfor a 2-D tensor it runs DOWN each column - unlike np.cumsum, which runs\nalong each row by default. Give an axis - t.cumsum 1, or -1 for the last\n- and a dim box appears so it can be changed.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.cumsum\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or NumPy array is turned into a float32 tensor). Every input is summed and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send. An axis the tensor does not have sends nothing.\n\ndim (int box, only with an argument):\nWhich axis to run along. Negative counts from the end. Changing it does not send by itself; the next tensor uses it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe running totals, the same shape as the input. The last value is the total of the whole run (what t.sum would give for a 1-D tensor).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: t.rand makes 200 random steps between -1 and 1, a new set every half second. The upper plot shows them - noise, with no shape. The lower plot is their cumsum: a random walk, the path you get by taking those steps one after another. Drag t.rand's max up to 1.2 (or min down) and the steps are no longer balanced: the walk drifts steadily up (or down).\n\nBottom: ten 1s summed give the ramp 1 2 3 ... 10 - the cumsum of a constant is a straight line whose slope is the constant. Edit the message (2 2 2, or 1 -1 1 -1) and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.cumsum is the NumPy version. t.diff is the reverse: it turns a path back into its steps. t.cumprod multiplies instead of adding. t.sum gives only the final total. accumulate keeps a running total of a STREAM, one number at a time."
        },
